nds or the salaries  of public officials but that in fact we  would try to open up all the data that  could power things like the innovations  that we are talking about today and then  go  beyond the idea of just a policy of just  words the creation of platforms that  actually allow us to point to put data  somewhere to have a place for people to  find that data so that we can go beyond  the promise and make the practice real  in principle there's great things that  it's allowing us to do in terms of  transforming how government works so  when they put up the school spending  data in Mexico on mejora tu escuela we  can see right away that fifteen hundred  and twelve people all on the payroll all  have the same birth date they're all a  hundred and two years old and they all  make more than the President of Mexico  so it allows us to yes improve  government accountability but it also in  the end allows parents to make better  choices and ultimately it's not a story  about the data and it's not a story  about the platforms even as we know and  as you Sowell pointed out it's about the  communities that we build around the  data so in Rajasthan in India the group  mkss has for the last decade been taking  government spending data budget data and  painting it on the walls of villages and  holding readings theatrical performances  in which they read the budget if that  doesn't sound exciting I don't know what  does but what they do is then they ask  people where is the bridge built to  nowhere where is the person who's died  who's still on the payroll and through  that mechanism using data as the raw  material co-create the process of  co-create the process and the practice  of greater accountability in government  so I want to just spend a couple of  minutes on talking about another sort of  development you're all very familiar  with kind of the movement around open  data the one though that I want to  emphasize that we have to continue to  fight for we cannot especially now take  it as a given or take it for granted the  two leading countries the US and the UK  that spearheaded this movement have both  had a change of administration neither  of which seemed poised to invest in this  agenda that's a best-case scenario is  that they ignore it but we all know that  the reality is that we might see  significant rollbacks on the opening up  of data and investments in open data  since it costs real money to actually do  it well we  are seeing though is a rise of what many  people call social corporate data  philanthropy or what we call data  collaborative Bloomberg just had a big  conference on data science for social  good in which many companies came  committing to open up their data and we  know that part of the clinical trials  story part of what so many people in  this room have been fighting for is to  not only get the clinical trials data  from NIH but to also get it directly  from the companies that are producing  this data so you know it's Twitter  providing its firehose of data to the  University of Walla Gong in Australia  and then using that the university using  that to create flood maps to help  promote resilience excuse me in  Indonesia so we're seeing lots of ways  in which open data including data that  comes from government data that comes  from researchers data that comes from  companies through collaboration often  between these three parties that's what  we call data collaboratives it's at that  intersection that a lot of exciting work  is happening I only have a few minutes  today to talk about this so I would  point you to a website that we've  created called OD impact open datas  impact where we're collecting case  studies and in-depth stories but also  then analysis of these examples of open  data we have another project with USAID  focused on those that work in the  development context as well that's all  going up here and I might add to your  point this is fully open source and  replicable and a number of people have  already forked this to create their own  archives and repositories of open data  impact stories since so much of this  work as you said earlier is about  telling the story of impact not just  talking about the promise so I want to  fly through a couple of examples that  again  I think for you this is a room that  needs no convincing but I think it's  important to at least share a little bit  of the story about the ways in which we  are transforming not only science but  we're transforming government and other  sectors through open data and then to  end with a couple of just thoughts on  the policy side of this so obviously we  can do lots of great stuff now in terms  of our ability to provide services  especially to vulnerable people by  monitor  populations in real-time so when we open  up things like the National Oceanic and  Atmospheric data we can do things like  better predict the weather and a lot of  that data though some of its coming from  the government as we know but some of  that data also then comes from companies  so it's Intel opening up data that  collects off of sensors that it's using  and the combination of data is allowing  us to create new tools that help to  promote resilience we can as we saw with  the mejora to escuela example also  improve how government spends money and  government efficiency and government  effectiveness as was mentioned I'm on  the board of a non-profit called the  open contracting partnership with works  with governments around the world -  opening up open up their contracting and  spending data by the way the data is  open but we need people to look at it  and use it if we're going to actually  spot the fraud waste and abuse at the  very least what it's starting to do is  to decrease a culture of corruption in  places like Slovakia the Ukraine which  interestingly is very out ahead in last  year on open contracting data and Mexico  and elsewhere but we're seeing this move  towards government using its own data 