 there  is no administrative state  infrastructure there isn't that data  being collected by government and so we  need to depend on the work of groups  like real impact analytics and Twitter  and others who have the data and push  them to make it available so that we can  do this kind of social good work more  efficiently and effectively my lab as a  partnership with UNICEF over the next  year and a half will be working we have  both worked to define the problems that  UNICEF works on in terms of improving  children's lives and then trying to find  the partners that we can turn to in  countries that don't have a census that  don't have a data collection  infrastructure or where we might a  create one using citizen scientists or  be use data that companies are anyway  collecting finally the last example that  I want to give and it's an example that  we frequently use when we have a hard  time convincing people of the need to  open up their data we can see how it  works with Provost's I know how it works  with Prime Minister's which is you tell  them not that open data is the right  thing to do you don't tell them about  solving social problems and helping  flood victims and and babies and  children you tell them it'll create jobs  and promote economic growth you tell  them about how New York City is using  open data to create a business Atlas  that gives the small business owner the  same kind of market research information  about where to site  jewelry store or their coffee shop as  the Starbucks or the gap has we have a  project called the open data 500 which  has been tracking and this by the way is  open ADA 500 is in the US and then we  have a bunch of other countries that  were partnered with labs and other  places tracking businesses that use open  data to create jobs and we're working  with scholars at Columbia Business  School to track the investment in those  businesses and their research shows that  in fact Oh business is driven by open  data perhaps surp