these benefits  it's not though all a story about open  data right and we know very well  particularly for people who are doing  human subjects research who are doing  biomedical research this is also a story  about the Fifty Shades of Grey in  between closed and open namely  responsible data access and data  governance knowing how to share data in  reliable and responsible ways so I was  very proud that my students yesterday  got to present a poster on work that  they're doing with two jurisdictions now  in Lafayette Parish Louisiana and  Cottington County South Dakota on their  governance practices for sharing data  between their criminal justice and  mental health agencies helping them to  work through that process and develop a  toolkit and kind of a map for how to do  that so they can in turn reduce  incarceration by diverting the mentally  ill out of jail or prison and into  treatment that can only be done through  data sharing but that has to be done in  ways they're legally compliant that are  ethically compliant where they actually  know what I T to use unlike one of the  jurisdictions which simply emails a  spreadsheet of everybody from one agency  to another which we know is not the best  way to do it but is so there's a whole  range of issues that intertwine the  technical and the legal and the ethical  that are really questions of data  governance that are at the center of a  lot of the work that we see happening  there's fabulous work being done now by  the UN's Food Lab and the UN hunger  program on collecting data about Syrian  refugees and their consumption of food  in the camps but they can't simply  publish and visualize this data less  they make the people they're trying to  help potential targets and victims of  crime and a  so there are lots of difficult ethical  questions that people are debating and  in this country where we have launched  we announced it last year and about a  hundred and twenty years days ago the  checks were dispersed in the precision  medicine initiative we have this great  hope that we will start to collect  longitudinal data from a million people  not just about their genetics but now  about their cultural practices their  environmental practices what they do and  to do that data to collect it  effectively we have to know responsibly  how to handle it we it's great if we  have ten years of data on people that  can allow us to quickly spot that I  might be at the risk of diabetes but  it's terrible if by virtue of that  surveillance I suddenly start getting  coupons for Big Macs then we're doing  something wrong we're doing something  wrong if we're not then also putting the  people at the center of the process and  ensuring that in that research we are  prioritizing the needs and the  conditions of the people who are most  underserved and underrepresented in  biomedical research I'm glad to report  it that in the precision medicine  initiative they have taken extra steps  to create a national level IRB with a  high degree of diversity in that IRB to  ensure exactly that the people and their  privacy is putting put at the center so  let me stop because I want to make sure  we have some time for questions and just  say that as we talk about all the  fabulous things that we can do with big  data in the sciences that we must put  the policies of privacy of openness of  ethical and responsible sharing and of  governance at the center and so with all  that I make a case for the lawyers and  why we're very happy to be part of this  conversation thank you  [Applause]
 

